{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"35c7e9f6e981a55d","name":"card_event_emergency_measures","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Legions/events/card_event_emergency_measures","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"EMERGENCY MEASURES","text":"Choose and discard a Spitfire to add a RAF GROUND CREW to the support line.","flavorText":"During the Battle of Britain there were several instances where pilots used their aircraft to ram into enemy aircraft. This was most of the time used as a last resort, when the ammo had run out or the guns were jammed, and many pilots never lived to tell the tale. There were three types of ramming: \r\n\r\n> Using the whole aircraft to ram directly into the enemy aircraft, this was of course the most dangerous option. \r\n> Using the propeller to chop off the controls in the tail of the enemy aircraft.\r\n> Using the wings, either to damage the enemy aircraft or to make it lose control.\r\n\r\nAnother method, which is not exactly ramming, was used by British pilots when intercepting the German V1 rockets and is called the “wing tipping”. Instead of trying to shoot at the V1 rocket, which involved the risk of being incinerated in the blast, the pilot would dive from high altitude in order to gain enough speed to catch up with the rocket, then fly beside it and tip the wing into in order to knock it off its course.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_emergency_measures","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_emergency_measures_title","SourceString":"EMERGENCY MEASURES","LocalizedString":"EMERGENCY MEASURES"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_emergency_measures_text","SourceString":"Choose and discard a Spitfire to add a RAF GROUND CREW to the support line.","LocalizedString":"Choose and discard a Spitfire to add a RAF GROUND CREW to the support line."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_emergency_measures_history","SourceString":"During the Battle of Britain there were several instances where pilots used their aircraft to ram into enemy aircraft. This was most of the time used as a last resort, when the ammo had run out or the guns were jammed, and many pilots never lived to tell the tale. There were three types of ramming: \r\n\r\n> Using the whole aircraft to ram directly into the enemy aircraft, this was of course the most dangerous option. \r\n> Using the propeller to chop off the controls in the tail of the enemy aircraft.\r\n> Using the wings, either to damage the enemy aircraft or to make it lose control.\r\n\r\nAnother method, which is not exactly ramming, was used by British pilots when intercepting the German V1 rockets and is called the “wing tipping”. Instead of trying to shoot at the V1 rocket, which involved the risk of being incinerated in the blast, the pilot would dive from high altitude in order to gain enough speed to catch up with the rocket, then fly beside it and tip the wing into in order to knock it off its course.","LocalizedString":"During the Battle of Britain there were several instances where pilots used their aircraft to ram into enemy aircraft. This was most of the time used as a last resort, when the ammo had run out or the guns were jammed, and many pilots never lived to tell the tale. There were three types of ramming: \r\n\r\n> Using the whole aircraft to ram directly into the enemy aircraft, this was of course the most dangerous option. \r\n> Using the propeller to chop off the controls in the tail of the enemy aircraft.\r\n> Using the wings, either to damage the enemy aircraft or to make it lose control.\r\n\r\nAnother method, which is not exactly ramming, was used by British pilots when intercepting the German V1 rockets and is called the “wing tipping”. Instead of trying to shoot at the V1 rocket, which involved the risk of being incinerated in the blast, the pilot would dive from high altitude in order to gain enough speed to catch up with the rocket, then fly beside it and tip the wing into in order to knock it off its course."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","waitWithOnOtherCardPlayedUntilHandTargetSelected":true,"selectHandCardNotifyText":{"Namespace":"","Key":"C69545C2481202FE7CF29A8688AC27B4","SourceString":"Chosen card must be a Spitfire","LocalizedString":"Chosen card must be a Spitfire"},"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Legions","isReserved":true,"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_3.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Britain/Invasion/t_spitfire.t_spitfire","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Alamy Stock Photo","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/EMERGENCY_MEASURES.EMERGENCY_MEASURES","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":487.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_emergency_measures_photo","SourceString":"RAF Spitfire trying to deflect a V-1 rocket from its target.","LocalizedString":"RAF Spitfire trying to deflect a V-1 rocket from its target."}},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_emergency_measures_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"targetedCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"out"}],"pure":false,"event":true,"flags":"FUNC_Event | FUNC_Public | FUNC_HasOutParms | FUNC_HasDefaults | FUNC_BlueprintCallable | FUNC_BlueprintEvent","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32"